WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.680
There are plenty of twists in Spider-Man:
Far From Home, and with everything going on,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.760
there were bound to be a few questions that
got tangled up in the web of illusion.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.820
From cliffhangers to dangling plot threads
to reveals with massive implications for the

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.940
future of the MCU, here are the biggest unanswered
questions in Spider-Man: Far From Home.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:20.279
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.599
The after-credits scene in Spider-Man: Far
From Home drops several bombs in quick succession

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.680
and introduces multiple questions about the
future of the MCU.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.180
The scene sees Nick Fury and Maria Hill driving,
when - surprise - they turn into Skrulls.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.420
Well, technically, the Skrulls who were masquerading
as Fury and Hill reveal their true forms.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.960
It turns out that for the entire movie, the
Skrulls Soren and Talos were covering for

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.580
the former S.H.I.E.L.D. agents.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.880
Talos then calls someone to report on everything
that just went down in Europe, and we see

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:55.550
that he's talking to none other than Nick
Fury himself, who's on some sort of spaceship

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.980
or high-tech base, manned by Skrulls.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.380
But where is he?

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.860
Well, while the film doesn't give any concrete
answers, there's one possibility that would

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.720
open up a whole new world.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.960
Now that Far From Home has officially closed
the door on MCU Phase Three, we're on the

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.720
cusp of a future where the MCU gets a lot
bigger and more cosmic.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.479
A bigger universe means bigger threats, and
what's poor little planet Earth to do in the

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.820
face of intergalactic alien invaders?

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.740
Easy: Build a big space station to protect
the world from extraterrestrial threats.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.900
Fortunately, someone's already done that:
S.W.O.R.D., or the Sentient World Observation

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:36.990
and Response Department, which debuted in
Astonishing X-Men in 2004 and went on to appear

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.160
in issues of Captain Marvel and Secret Invasion,
to name a few comic titles.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.940
It's basically S.H.I.E.L.D. in space, and
it operated as a subdivision of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.520
Is that where Nick Fury was?

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:51.240
There's nothing in the film to confirm it,
but if the post-credits scene of Far From

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.840
Home did in fact introduce S.W.O.R.D., it's
kind of a big deal.

00:01:55.009 --> 00:02:00.159
See, since it debuted in an X-Men comic, the
film rights to the agency have long been mired

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.780
in the whole Fox/Marvel legal rights struggle.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.549
Now that Disney's acquisition of Fox has gone
through, however, that years-long question

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:11.870
of who owns what is a moot point, and the
introduction of S.W.O.R.D. may just mark one

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:15.380
of the first steps in bringing parts of these
different universes together.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.520
Spider-Man: Far From Home's plot is all about
how appearances can be deceiving.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.300
Its central villain, Mysterio, uses complex
holograms to trick the world into thinking

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.390
monsters are coming out of the ground, and
he puts that tech to good use in his battles

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.720
with Spidey, plunging our hero into a world
where nothing is what it seems.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:34.840
He's full of tricks.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.520
"And now our magic trick."

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.120
"Illusion."

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.520
"SILENCE SLAVE"

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.160
Paranoia and illusion are all pieces of the
puzzle here, which makes the end-credits reveal

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.900
that Maria Hill and Nick Fury were Skrulls
the whole time so perfect.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.040
But that also raises another question with
even bigger ramifications.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.500
Fury has known about, and potentially been
working with, the Skrulls since the '90s,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.320
the decade when Captain Marvel is set.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.940
Despite Carol promising to find them a new
home, some Skulls apparently hung around on

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:06.960
Earth, and Fury clearly has no more qualms
about letting them impersonate important people.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:10.860
Does this mean we should expect other characters
to turn out to be secret Skrulls?

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.810
Hopefully not, but now that the cat's out
of the bag, anything is possible.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.440
Like Tom Holland himself, Peter Parker is
terrible at keeping secrets.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.500
Far From Home marks the second solo Spidey
movie in the MCU, and it also marks the second

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.950
solo Spidey movie in the MCU where the main
bad guy knows Peter's real identity.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.060
"I'll kill you and everybody you love."

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.300
In the mid-credits scene, Mysterio reveals
Spider-Man's identity to the world, which

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.940
is one of Peter's biggest fears.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:45.340
He just watched Quentin Beck target his closest
friends with murder drones, so he has first-hand

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.450
knowledge of the dangers of having your secret
identity exposed.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:03.220
Now that the whole world knows who he is,
his life is basically open season for any

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:05.840
bad guy who may want a piece of the Spider
pie.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.540
We know from Homecoming that Scorpion's itching
to get back at Spider-Man.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.140
Who else might join the bug hunt?

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.300
All his friends and loved ones are in danger,
and even his home is no longer safe.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:19.920
Clearly, the reveal was intended as a cliffhanger
for a third Spider-Man film, and it's the

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.180
kind of question that could lead in any direction.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.800
For a movie about Spider-Man, Far From Home
sure spends a lot of time focusing on Iron

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.300
Man.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.140
Specifically, on the vacancy Tony Stark left
behind.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:36.120
There's a hole in the Avengers roster, a hole
in Peter's heart, and a hole in the world

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.000
where the Armored Avenger used to stand.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.580
Peter himself grapples with the question of
whether he is - or at least is supposed to

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:43.810
be - the next Iron Man.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.640
"You gonna be the next Iron Man now?

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.700
Well no I don't have time; I'm too busy doing
your job.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:48.380
Ooooh.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:49.520
I'm kidding, I'm kidding."

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.460
He even gets into the spirit of Tony when
he gets to work building his new suit while

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:57.750
listening to his favorite Led Zeppelin song,
AC/DC's "Back in Black."

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.980
But ultimately, there is no answer to be found
in Spider-Man: Far From Home.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.210
The film builds the question to a crescendo,
then leaves it hanging there, presumably for

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.740
a future MCU installment.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.400
Darn you and your endless sequels, Marvel!

